WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.598
A request for an account balance reaches a Quarkus service.

2
00:00:06.598 --> 00:00:11.817
Every request is read on a Vert.x event loop, which Quarkus calls an IO thread.

3
00:00:11.817 --> 00:00:14.328
One IO thread serves many connections.

4
00:00:14.328 --> 00:00:16.508
So which thread runs your method?

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.678
Quarkus REST decides from your method's signature.

6
00:00:19.678 --> 00:00:21.776
This one returns a plain Balance.

7
00:00:21.776 --> 00:00:22.920
Where does it run?

8
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.333
On a worker thread.

9
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:35.159
The IO thread hands the call to a bounded pool, 200 threads on a 4-core machine, and goes straight back to its other connections.

10
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.262
This one returns a Uni.

11
00:00:37.262 --> 00:00:38.588
Where does it run?

12
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.301
Right on the IO thread, with no hop.

13
00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:48.721
A Uni, or a Kotlin suspend function, is a promise: this method won't block.

14
00:00:48.721 --> 00:00:50.021
Quarkus trusts it.

15
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:54.247
Now someone wraps a JDBC call in a Uni, to make it reactive.

16
00:00:54.247 --> 00:00:56.916
What happens to the other connections?

17
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:02.089
findById runs on the IO thread, and blocks it.

18
00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:04.879
Every connection on that event loop waits.

19
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.931
After 2 seconds, Quarkus logs a warning with the stack trace.

20
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:15.487
On 4 cores, Quarkus runs 4 event loops, so one blocked loop stalls a quarter of your connections.

21
00:01:15.487 --> 00:01:20.012
Keep the plain return type, add @Blocking, or use a reactive client.

22
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.665
The third choice is @RunOnVirtualThread.

23
00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:28.552
Your code still blocks, but on a new virtual thread, so waiting doesn't hold a pooled thread.

24
00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:40.431
In the lesson's demo, 200 calls of 100 milliseconds each took about a second on a pool of 20 workers, and 113 milliseconds on virtual threads.

25
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.920
Here are the three signatures, in Java and in Kotlin.

26
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.766
So the signature picks the thread.

27
00:01:50.766 --> 00:01:55.832
Uni or suspend promises not to block, and Quarkus runs it on the event loop.

28
00:01:55.832 --> 00:01:59.699
Blocking code belongs on a worker, or on a virtual thread.

29
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.664
Quick check.

30
00:02:00.664 --> 00:02:04.911
A Kotlin suspend endpoint calls a JDBC repository directly.

31
00:02:04.911 --> 00:02:10.167
Which thread runs it, and what do the other connections on that loop see?
